module v2.workflow.floor_expected_red

import v2.std.algebra { Cons, Empty, filter, list_flat_map }
import v2.std.collection { List }

// 101 ROWS LEFT THIS ROSTER ON 2026-08-20 AND WERE NEVER ELIGIBLE FOR IT — a fact about this
// mechanism, not those rows, so it is recorded at the top where the next enrolling author reads it.
//
// They were reclassified into `v2.workflow.floor_route_gap`. Each already executed every run and
// reached a HERMETIC REFUSAL — a host effect the hermetic route has no arm for — and because that
// refusal arrived as a `TypeError` carrying prose rather than a typed outcome, `ExpectedRedArm`
// read it as an ordinary failure and HELD it as agreement. So this roster asserted "runs, fails,
// someone is fixing it" about 101 identities that NEVER REACHED THEIR SUBJECT. Nothing was fixing
// them: they needed a ROUTE, not a REPAIR.
//
// THE ARITHMETIC MAKES IT A FINDING RATHER THAN A SUSPICION. Floor run 32345970386 carried 308
// enrolled identities and held 207; the 101 difference is exactly — not approximately — the set
// of enrolled identities that route-gapped.
//
// THE LESSON, previously unstated here: ENROLLING AN IDENTITY ASSERTS THAT IT REACHES ITS SUBJECT
// AND ANSWERS. A row that cannot run is not debt this roster can carry, and it is the more
// dangerous mistake: a genuine red is loud once someone looks, while a never-executed row looks
// identical to progress. `ExpectedRedArm` now refuses to hold `HostEffectRefused`,
// `HostToolUnresolved`, and an interrupted budget outcome — three ways of producing no verdict,
// none of them agreement.
//
// THE EXPECTED-RED ROSTER: identities this branch KNOWS fail, enrolled by exact qualified name.
//
// This is NOT a skip list, and that distinction is its whole justification. Every identity here
// still EXECUTES in the fold and its outcome is still asserted; the roster only changes which
// outcome counts as agreement. Enrolled and fails: agreement. NOT enrolled and fails: reds the
// build. Enrolled and PASSES: also reds the build, naming itself for removal — so a fix is
// counted the moment it lands instead of being absorbed into a green run.
//
// That last arm makes this monotone. A skip list grows quietly and rots, because nothing
// observes its members or notices when they start working. This roster can only shrink under an
// honest run, and its length is the debt.
//
// WHY IT EXISTS. The floor cut replaced an evaluator running a selected subset per PR with one
// folding the whole discovered roster through a single prepared subject. The replacement is
// faster end to end, runs strictly more rows, and fails a population the old one did not — some
// this branch broke, some the old floor never executed. Holding the branch until all are repaired
// keeps everyone on the slower, less complete floor; enrolling them keeps the new floor's verdict
// honest while they are worked.
//
// WHAT IT MUST NOT BECOME. A row belongs here only while someone is fixing it. It is not a
// disposition: it names no executing consumer other than this floor, establishes no coverage,
// and an identity sitting here indefinitely is a defect nobody owns wearing a receipt. The roster
// is admissible because it is temporary, visible at identity grain, and self-emptying — remove
// any of the three and it is the skip list it is carefully not.
//
// THE PRESENT POPULATION IS NOT WRITTEN HERE, AND THAT IS THE CORRECTION, NOT AN OMISSION. It is
// `floor_expected_red_roster()` — the fold over the chunks below — and that fold is the only
// authority. A transcribed total is a SECOND representation of a fact this module computes
// (DESIGN 2/3): nothing derives from the sentence, so nothing refuses when it goes stale, and it
// goes stale on the roster's own removal path, which fires without anyone editing this header.
//
// It had already gone stale twice over: this block carried TWO current-state totals — `311` here
// and `306` immediately below — each correct when written, neither correct together, and the
// roster had moved on from both. Two accounts of one population in one comment is two
// authorities for one fact; the repair is to delete both, not reconcile them into a third number
// that rots on the same schedule.
//
// WHAT SURVIVES: a DATED MEASUREMENT bound to a named run is a receipt — its subject is a fixed
// tree, so it stays true forever. A BARE PRESENT-TENSE TOTAL is a claim about the live roster,
// which this file already computes. So the derivations, run ids, the 429/313 partition and the
// removal history below remain untouched; only the two sentences asserting a current count are
// gone.
//
// The five rows added 2026-08-22 are the previously-INVISIBLE rows described at chunk_20 — they
// enter from marker-less files rather than from a regression, a route this roster had not carried
// before, which is why they are called out here.
//
// The derivation below is a receipt and is unchanged: the declared rung drop took 111 out by
// relocation and run 32189201111 (PR #8460) observed 52 more PASSING (469 -> 361 -> 358 -> 306 as
// of that run). Those 52 reported STALE-QUARANTINE — enrolled as expected-red and PASSED — the
// roster's own removal path, not a roster edit: they pass because that branch closed two emitter
// roots (a builtin answering a call the program had already bound to a lexical binding, and a
// bare closure emitted where an arrow return was declared), so witnesses red against those
// defects now agree. That run named 57; the other 5 were already removed on main when it landed.
// The count is NOT the whole affected population; the rest is stated exactly here: 38 modules
// were relocated carrying 429 `test fn` declarations. THE PARTITION MUST BE DISJOINT, and an
// earlier revision read "111 enrolled, 111 over-budget, 207 healthy", which sums to 429 only by
// counting the 102 rows BOTH enrolled AND over budget on both sides. Enrolment and cost are
// independent axes, so the partition is taken on enrolment with the cost facts inside each part:
//
//   111 enrolled here and removed (the 469 -> 358 delta), which the pre-quarantine run
//       32177951514 partitions exactly -- 469 = 367 held red + 97 budget-refused + 5 passed
//       over budget -- so the 111 are 9 held red, 97 budget-refused, and all 5 passed-over-budget.
//     5 unenrolled and budget-refused.
//
//   97 AND 102 ARE BOTH CORRECT AND NAME DIFFERENT SETS, which made earlier accounts read as
//   contradictory; neither may be written as a bare `budget_refused`:
//     budget-interrupted identities, global                    102
//       enrolled here AND budget-interrupted                    97
//       unenrolled AND budget-interrupted                        5
//     enrolled here AND completed-over-budget (stale_quarantine)  5
//   313 passing when relocated, enrolled nowhere, counted by no number in this file. NINE later
//       tipped over the ceiling once wave 1 removed the slow head (8 in 32186963581, 1 straggler
//       in 32193032348), so 304 have never been observed over budget. THE EIGHT ARE 32186963581,
//       not 32189985063; the counters decide it without prose: 32186963581 failed=8
//       known_red_held=361, 32189985063 failed=1 known_red_held=358, 32193032348 failed=1
//       known_red_held=358. Only one run observed eight. The wrong id survived three rewrites
//       of this block while the SECOND WAVE paragraph below named the right one throughout --
//       one file, two accounts of one population, which is why the run counters and not the
//       neighbouring sentence are the authority.
//
//   111 + 5 + 313 = 429. Independently: `grep -c '^test fn'` over the 38 relocated modules is
//   also 429, and differencing the two floor runs gives the same split (below).
//
// THE PROBLEM POPULATION IS NOT ONE NUMBER EITHER: 107 rows were over budget or failing at
// quarantine time (102 budget-refused + 5 passed over budget), 116 including the 9 that tipped
// afterwards, and 102 is the interrupted rows alone.
//
// These figures are checkable against the tree and the run counters, and are stated because the
// earlier revision said 361 while the roster carried 358, said 108 where the delta is 111, and
// called its collateral "roughly 200". A bounded population is the one thing a rung drop must not
// round: sharp-raven-273's monotone-shrink wall reads this carrier as its baseline, so a stale
// count here is load-bearing for work that is not mine. The 358/111 corrections are
// tidy-lark-471's external review; the disjointness and per-part cost split are royal-hawk-392's.
//
// HISTORY: cut at 820 on tree 380c242c79a; 25 removed after the first complete run on main
// (611fd027708) observed them PASSING and red the build naming themselves — the removal path
// working as designed. 164 more removed once prepared-floor scopes began binding bare helper
// names by precedence instead of corpus order, and 33 more after run 32162750949 observed them
// PASSING on the merge ref — the same path, twice more. EVERY removal is an OBSERVED pass, never
// an inference: a row reported BUDGET-REFUSED is UNDECIDED and stays enrolled, because the
// floor's own diagnostic is right that removing it would not help when it is not passing either.
// The count is a fact about that exact tree only — this branch's diagnostic sensitivity is not
// monotone across trees, so these names may not be comparable to a count on main or an earlier
// head. Each is enrolled at exact qualified-name grain, never by prefix, module, or directory,
// so enrolling one row can never silently enroll a neighbour.
//
// DECLARED RUNG DROP (DESIGN 4b), 2026-08-18, operator-directed, temporary.
//
// PREVIOUS RUNG: 469 enrolled identities, every one executing on the required floor each run.
// TEMPORARY RUNG: 361. One hundred and eight enrollments were removed here, not because any row
// was observed passing — the ONLY removal reason this roster otherwise admits — but because
// their modules were relocated to long/ homes and no longer execute at all.
//
// REASON: 102 witnesses could not finish inside required_floor_claim_budget_ms (1552ms), and
// reported across two channels that both exit nonzero (budget_refused, stale_quarantine). The
// floor could not deliver a verdict about the other 9,318 rows while they stood.
//
// BOUNDED POPULATION, both halves, because neither is visible to any mechanism in this tree:
//   108 enrollments removed here, being every identity in the 21 relocated modules. That is
//       NOT 102: exactly 102 are the cost rows (verified by identity join against main run
//       32177951514 -- all 102 removed, none missed), and SIX are enrolled rows behaving exactly
//       as enrolled (known_red_held) that leave only because they share a module with a
//       relocated row. Named, because a population described as 102 while being 108 is not
//       bounded at the grain it claims:
//         gunbc.test.claim.realization_sweep_test.witness_canonical_identities_join_own_enrolled_roster
//         v2.test.execution.add_arrow_eval_by_execution.add_arrow_eval_direct_runtime_holds
//         v2.test.execution.add_arrow_eval_by_execution.add_arrow_eval_generality_executes_holds
//         v2.test.execution.add_arrow_eval_by_execution.add_arrow_eval_lazy_arm_branch_suppression_holds
//         v2.test.execution.add_arrow_eval_by_execution.add_arrow_eval_produced_add_body_executes_holds
//         v2.test.execution.add_arrow_eval_by_execution.add_arrow_eval_source_driven_add_executes_holds
//       These six are a THIRD collateral class, distinct from the 78: those are unenrolled and
//       passing, these were enrolled and correctly red. Both leave because a module is the unit
//       of relocation, and both return when their module does.
//    78 healthy, fast, PASSING witnesses that left the floor as collateral, sharing a file with a
//       slow row. Worst offenders: roadmap_page_witness 29, live_read_classification 17,
//       add_arrow_eval_by_execution 13.
// Nothing refuses on their absence. Nothing counts them. That is why they are written here.
//
// A JOIN AGAINST THE DEFERRAL FREEZE, AND THE INFERENCE I DREW FROM IT WAS WRONG (recorded
// 2026-08-19, corrected the same day by still-wolf-510 against a live run). The join is real:
// this roster and gunbc.witness_deferral_freeze name the same identities in 38-40 places
// depending on the head. My conclusion — that those rows can never leave because nothing
// executes them, so the self-emptying rule can never fire — IS FALSE, an assumption about a
// mechanism I did not read.
//
// The required floor excludes by AUTHORED MODULE NAME via required_floor long_home_prefixes and
// does not consult the freeze at all. The freeze is an ADMISSION standing — whether a row may
// remain without an executing consumer — and the floor gate tolerates LegacyFrozenPathDeferral;
// it never suppresses execution. A frozen path and a discovered module are independent facts,
// and an identity can be both.
//
// MEASURED, on green main run 32201531653: planned=9022 executed=9022 passed=8664
// known_red_held=358 failed=0, and the intersection's own members appear in that output —
// test.claim.ci_exclusion_proof.ci_exclusion_proof_sentinel reports FAILED in 13640ns, a row
// executing and held exactly as enrolled. These rows are evaluated every run and can leave the
// moment they pass.
//
// SO THE DEFECT IS THE OTHER WAY ROUND, smaller and mechanical: the freeze rows are STALE. They
// assert path-deferral for identities that have an executing consumer — the required floor — so
// the honest disposition is to retire those freeze rows with a receipt, not to find the roster
// rows a cadence. The construction wall still earns its place: the intersection is a
// contradiction whichever way it is resolved, and nothing detects it today.
//
// KEPT RATHER THAN QUIETLY REWRITTEN because the error is this document's own subject: I joined
// two carriers correctly and then read one carrier's meaning off its name instead of its
// mechanism — exactly the stale-citation class DESIGN section 3 names — while correcting three
// other people's counts in the same file.
//
// WHAT THIS IS NOT, because the shape is dangerous: NOT the self-emptying rule. Every other
// removal came from OBSERVING a pass. These rows were removed by relocation, and a roster that
// silently went 469 -> 361 is indistinguishable, to every mechanism in the tree, from the defect
// that dropped 197 identities on 2026-08-18 and went unnoticed for an hour. An empty roster now
// refuses; a shrinking one still does not.
//
// NOT A PRECEDENT, because the next reader will see a contradiction that is not there.
// `required_floor` states that relocating a file does not discharge a budget refusal, and that
// stands unweakened: an AUTHOR moving their own over-budget witness to a long home deletes the
// coverage while retaining the source — the gunbc#7762 specimen the 2026-08-04 admission ruling
// exists for. This is a different act: an operator-ruled, bounded, declared rung-drop with a
// named population and a restoration trigger, which DESIGN 4b permits explicitly. The
// distinction is declaration and reversibility, never the directory. So this quarantine is not
// grounds to revert that comment or that refusal, and a relocation offered WITHOUT these five
// declared fields is still the unsanctioned move.
//
// SECOND WAVE, 2026-08-18, operator-directed at a 1000ms line, plus one measured straggler. The
// first wave quarantined what was RED. Run 32186963581 then proved both budget channels clear
// (stale_quarantine=0, budget_refused=0) and surfaced EIGHT rows that had been passing under the
// ceiling and tipped over once the slow head was gone — the marginal population, measured rather
// than predicted. Sixteen more modules leave: the fifteen over 1000ms, and
// test.claim.e0308_mechanical_trio_test at 774ms, below the line but FAILED on that run, so
// quarantined on evidence rather than threshold. Three more enrolled rows retire with them
// (roster 361 -> 358; then 358 -> 306 on the #8460 passes above), and further healthy siblings
// leave under the same module-grain rule as the 78 — counted exactly in the POPULATION block,
// never rounded. The ceiling is 1552ms, calibrated as measured-max-plus-one-quantum from a SINGLE
// 1527ms observation, so rows within a few hundred ms of it are not stably green on a shared
// runner; that is the real reason this wave exists.
//
// THIRD ROW, 2026-08-18: test.claim.emitted_lib_rs_module_declaration_witness_test, at 42545ms.
// Named separately because of HOW it was missed: my cost table came from a run where the 30s
// wall interrupted the longest rows, so every censored row reported its deadline, not its cost —
// 30000ms, not 42545ms. The table under-reported exactly the rows most needing quarantine, and
// this module fell outside a 1000ms cut it exceeds by 42x. The measurement was a lower bound and
// I read it as a value. Its 3 witnesses leave with it; no roster row named it, so the relocation
// left the roster at 358 (306 after the #8460 removals).
//
// THE SECOND DERIVATION, kept because a coverage-loss figure asserted once is exactly what this
// declaration exists to prevent. Differencing the two floor runs on main reproduces the split
// from run counters alone, without reading the tree:
//   pre  32177951514 @59653f569e  planned=9420 passed=8951 known_red=367 stale_quarantine=5 budget_refused=102
//   post 32193032348 @6f3d374de0  planned=8997 passed=8638 known_red=358 stale_quarantine=0 budget_refused=0
//   passed 313 + known_red 9 + budget_refused 102 + passed-over-budget 5 = 429.
// The 6-identity gap against `planned` (9420 - 8997 = 423, not 429) is six identities that
// LANDED between the runs, in the three commits separating those heads — not a discrepancy in
// this population, stated so 423 is not read as the answer.
//
// WHAT IS STILL NOT DERIVED: which INDIVIDUAL identities make up the 313. The figures are exact
// in aggregate and unavailable at identity grain, because the floor reports per-identity outcomes
// only as log lines and a scraped log is not an oracle. Closing that needs the floor to emit an
// identity-grained receipt, not a better scrape; this block is SUPPORTING EVIDENCE for that
// receipt, not a substitute.
//
// AND `budget_refused=0` ON THE POST RUN IS NOT THE POPULATION DISAPPEARING — the floor stopped
// executing it. The 116 identities ever observed over budget or failing are still owed a
// remeasurement, at identity grain, against a cause, not a green counter.
//
// A COST FIGURE HERE IS NOT A PROPERTY OF ITS WITNESS WHEN AN UNREPORTED LAZY SHARED FILL IS IN
// PLAY, and this quarantine proved it by accident.
// test.claim.emitter_nested_refinement_cast_witness_test
// single_refinement_carrier_emits_no_unsupported_cast cost 746ms on the pre run and 45941ms on
// the post run, unchanged in itself; its sibling in the same file costs 763ms. The relocated
// modules included every earlier caller of `compile_dag_rust_emit_check`, so the one-time
// `build_module_path_index_from_witness_roots` construction relocated onto the first surviving
// caller and that row became main's only failure.
//
// THE LAW IS NARROW, and stating it wider was this note's own first error (operator ruling,
// 2026-08-18): a restoration receipt is confounded WHEN CHANGING THE POPULATION CHANGES WHICH
// CLAIM PAYS AN UNREPORTED LAZY SHARED FILL — NOT that every restoration is confounded until
// every subject is prepared. Preparation is the remedy for THIS class — process-scoped lazy
// first-touch construction — and #8470 already applied it to the instance above by paying the
// module-path index in floor preparation. It is not the remedy for closure pollution,
// fixture/live-index coupling, repeated end-to-end crossings, duplicate coverage, true whole-tree
// censuses, or genuinely size-scaling algorithmic cost — separate classes with separate owning
// layers. The remaining lazy-fill members are what #8464's process-scoped cold-fill inventory is
// for, and they are the only ones this paragraph speaks about.
//
// RESTORATION TRIGGER: the cost lane. Measured by three sessions independently on 2026-08-18,
// the cost is the EVALUATION CONTEXT, not the witnesses — a row taking 71s on the floor runs in
// 70-85ms in isolation, and a 12s row in 31ms.
//
// ONE CLAUSE OF THAT PARAGRAPH IS REFUTED BY EXECUTION (quiet-bee-353, 2026-08-19, gunbc#8478,
// remote executor, release claim_batch). It read: "dag_arrow_lambda rebuilds a 6,230-line
// grammar once per witness for 3-line sources because each claim gets a fresh frame by design."
// Folding that file from nineteen grammar builds to two DID NOT PAY — 210.9s for the two folded
// claims against ~208s for the fourteen they replaced — so the fold was reverted and the file
// left byte-identical. Solving the terms out: a `dag_grammar()` construction is ~0.5s and a
// single three-line parse through the interpreted frontend is ~12s. The parse is the cost, not
// the apparatus. The arrow row must NOT be planned as a witness-side repair; twelve seconds to
// parse three lines is a frontend cost-shape defect plausibly behind several rows in this
// quarantine rather than an evaluation-context tax.
//
// SO "no witness has yet been found that is genuinely slow" IS NO LONGER ESTABLISHED, corrected
// rather than deleted because it was load-bearing for how the lane was planned: it licensed
// treating every relocated row as an apparatus problem. Still true: the two measured extremes
// above are apparatus. Not established: that the residue is. When a module's cost is repaired it
// returns to the floor WHOLE with its healthy siblings, so the collateral dissolves with work
// already scheduled. This declaration is deleted when the relocated population reaches zero.
//
// FIRST RESTORATION DECISION, 2026-08-19: NOT YET, and the module returns WHOLE when it does.
// Recorded because the session that declared this trigger is archived, so the next reader would
// otherwise find a trigger nobody holds and no account of what was already decided against.
//
// The evidence offered was dag_arrow_lambda_witness_test, the densest parse-bearing module in
// the relocated population: same instrument, flags and budget, merge base 2c65eeacf32 against a
// sync-token change. 12 of its 14 rows go 12237-14143ms to 193-555ms, 24x to 69x; the module
// total goes 228167ms to 27876ms; rows under the 1552ms ceiling go 0 of 14 to 12 of 14. The
// BEFORE column is the finding: fourteen rows parsing fourteen DIFFERENT sources agreed within
// 14% — not fourteen expensive witnesses but one defect wearing fourteen names.
//
// Refused on two grounds, neither of them doubt about that measurement.
//
// FIRST, the AFTER column was measured on an unmerged branch head. Restoring against it would
// baseline the roster on a tree that does not exist, and if the branch changes before landing
// the restoration rests on numbers nothing can reproduce. The trigger is repaired cost, and cost
// is repaired when the repair is IN main.
//
// SECOND, the trigger's own words: a module "returns to the floor WHOLE". Two of the fourteen
// are still over budget — the first row pays the surviving preparation, and pre_repair pays two
// because it builds a MODIFIED grammar and legitimately owes a second. Restoring twelve would
// split one file across two homes and mint a second module whose only membership rule is a cost
// threshold — the directory deciding an admission question (the 2026-08-04 ruling this file
// cites). The correct wait is for the first-row constant to fall, after which all fourteen clear
// together and the module moves once.
//
// AND THE MEASUREMENT THAT SHOULD PRECEDE THE NEXT ONE, for the other 37 modules: at least nine
// of the 38 were free riders that merely touched a shared corpus fill first, so quarantining
// them relocated the bill to the next module instead of removing it — watched changing hands on
// one cache key at identity grain. Floor observation noise is RELATIVE and flat at 4-5%, p95
// 13.5%, so the audit question for a quarantine decision is whether its observation sat within
// ~13.5% of the ceiling. Both decision runs behind the second wave clear that by a wide margin,
// at 27x and 30x the ceiling.

// Rows are carried in fixed-size chunks and flattened because one Cons chain 820 deep is a
// parse-depth hazard, not because chunk boundaries mean anything: a chunk is a slice of an
// alphabetical list and nothing reads it.
fn floor_expected_red_chunk_00() -> List<String> {
  Cons { head: "test.claim.ci_exclusion_proof.ci_exclusion_proof_sentinel", tail:     Empty {} }
}

